# loops is installed in this project

This project has `loops` installed at `.loops/` (per-project clone). Loops are portable workflow definitions that work in Cursor and Claude Code. When the user says "use the loops" or asks for help on a task, route through the dispatcher.

> **Global install alternative:** On machines that ran `node adapters/install-global.js`, loops are also available via `~/.loops` for every project. If this workspace has no `.loops/` directory, use `~/.loops` (or the absolute path in `~/.cursor/rules/loops.mdc`) instead of the `.loops/` paths below. Prefer project-local `.loops/` when both exist.

Below, `LOOPS_ROOT` means `.loops/` in this project (or `~/.loops` when using the global install).

## What to do

1. **When the user says "use the loops"** (or anything equivalent — "loops?", "pick a loop", "which loop should I use"), read `LOOPS_ROOT/dispatcher/loop.md` and follow it. It will tell you how to classify the request and load the right loop.

2. **When the user makes a request that maps to a known loop** (building a feature, writing tests, reviewing a PR, stress-testing a design, reproducing a bug, migrating, explaining the codebase, swarming a full pipeline), you can short-circuit the dispatcher: read the appropriate `LOOPS_ROOT/loops/<name>/loop.md` directly and follow it.

3. **When the user names a loop explicitly** ("run plan-and-implement", "do tdd on this", "swarm this"), read that loop's `loop.md` and follow it.

4. **If you're not sure** whether a request fits a loop, run the dispatcher. It will either pick one or route to `use-the-loop` which handles ambiguous cases. Explicit "swarm" keywords route to `swarm`, not `use-the-loop`.

## Available loops (in `LOOPS_ROOT/loops/`)

- `plan-and-implement` — Spec-then-implement for new features, refactors, non-trivial changes.
- `tdd` — Test-driven development. Lock in behavior with a test suite.
- `sar` — Spec → Attack → Repair. Adversarial review of candidates.
- `adversarial-gate` — Pre-merge review for a PR or branch.
- `reproduce-and-fix` — Minimal repro → failing test → fix → prove green.
- `migrate` — Version/framework upgrade with checklist and rollback.
- `explain-codebase` — Onboarding map for an unfamiliar repo.
- `swarm` — Mega-loop. Full beginning-to-end ship pipeline.
- `use-the-loop` — Meta-router. Smallest composition; open-ended tasks.

Personas under `LOOPS_ROOT/personas/` are review lenses for `sar` / `adversarial-gate`, not top-level loops. Those loops **Read** `personas/<name>.md` before each persona step.

## Discovery helpers (emitted, namespaced)

After `node LOOPS_ROOT/adapters/emit.js` (from a `.loops/` clone, emit writes into the **project** root):

- Cursor: `.cursor/rules/loops-*.mdc`
- Claude Code: `.claude/skills/loops-*/` (invoke `/loops-dispatcher`, etc.)
- Claude awareness: `.claude/rules/loops.md` (copy from `LOOPS_ROOT/INSTALL-CLAUDE.md` on per-project install)

The `loops-` prefix avoids colliding with other skills (e.g. Hermes `plan-and-implement`). Prefer reading canonical `LOOPS_ROOT/**/loop.md` when running a loop.

## Model selection

Phases declare `model_class` in each loop’s `loop.yaml`. **Single source of truth:** `LOOPS_ROOT/adapters/MODEL_CLASSES.md`. Prefer the strongest available non-Fable model; use Task/subagent for `high-reasoning` when available; fall back to `grok-4.5-xhigh` on Cursor usage limits. Never use Fable. Claude Code: classes are advisory.

## What NOT to do

- Don't read `LOOPS_ROOT` loop files just to "understand the project." Only load them when the user has triggered a loop.
- Don't invent new loops. If nothing fits, route to `use-the-loop`.
- Don't run loops without following the phases. Each loop's `loop.md` defines its own contract.
- Don't treat personas as dispatcher options.

## Re-emitting adapter files

If the user edits a canonical `loop.md` in `.loops/`, run:

```bash
node .loops/adapters/emit.js
```

This regenerates project `.cursor/rules/loops-*.mdc` and `.claude/skills/loops-*/` from the canonical source.

For a **global** install (all projects on this machine), refresh with:

```bash
cd ~/.loops && git pull && node adapters/emit.js && node adapters/install-global.js
```
